      "writtenOn": "2021-01-19T13:34:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Why does you need this even matter? AFAICS what we need to do is \"for upstream packets, if the src and dst addresses are in the same /64, don\u0027t offload\".\n\nWhat\u0027s the point of the extra check for 64:ff9b::? Why would we ever receive a packet on our downstream interface that has *both a source and a destination* in 64:ff9b::? Pretty much by definition that never happens - NAT64 translates from IPv6 (i.e., non-64:ff9b::) to IPv4 (i.e., 64:ff9b::) or vice versa.\n\nAlso - Android does support networks where the NAT64 prefix is not 64:ff9b::. So if this code is necessary, we should probably add a TODO for making the NAT64 prefix configurable.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 77,
        "startChar": 23,
        "endLine": 77,
        "endChar": 51
